== no_such_variable
error[Z0101]: no variable `fited` in `d`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:36
  |
3 | d.plot(x: educ, y: wage).points(y: fited)
  |                                    ^^^^^ not a variable of d
  |
  = help: did you mean `fitted`? If you meant a category level, write it in quotes: `"fitted"`

== another_case
error[Z0102]: no variable `Fitted` in `d`; names are case-sensitive
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:36
  |
3 | d.plot(x: educ, y: wage).points(y: Fitted)
  |                                    ^^^^^^ differs only in case from `fitted`
  |
  = help: write `fitted`

== not_a_number
error[Z0401]: the plot places `wage` on a linear scale along `y`, but `industry`, which `points` maps to `y`, is a Category, which is placed as categories
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:36
  |
3 | d.plot(x: educ, y: wage).points(y: industry)
  |                                    ^^^^^^^^ `industry` is of another kind
  |
  = help: the layers of a plot share one axis, so what is mapped to `y` must all be categories (a Str or a Category), all be numbers or all be dates (a Date or a DateTime); `category(v)` makes categories of a number

== no_such_variable_in_an_expression
error[Z0101]: no variable `wagee` in `d`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:40
  |
3 | d.plot(x: educ, y: wage).points(y: log(wagee))
  |                                        ^^^^^ not a variable of d
  |
  = help: did you mean `wage`? If you meant a category level, write it in quotes: `"wage"`

== one_value_for_all_rows
error[Z0401]: an expression mapped to an aesthetic cannot use `mean`, a function of `data`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:3:36
  |
3 | d.plot(x: educ, y: wage).points(y: mean(wage))
  |                                    ^^^^ not kept in a mapping
  |
  = help: a mapping is computed from variables, numbers, the operators and the functions of `core`, as in `log(wage)` or `wage / hours`; compute anything else with `add` first and map the new variable

== not_in_its_own_frame
